-- Seed for the SSCC bylaws workspace: v1.0 = the clean revision draft
-- (one member's proposed rewrite of the 2022 bylaws, South_Shore_Cultural_Center_Advisory_Council_Clean_Bylaws_Revision.docx).
-- Run once, after sscc-bylaws-schema.sql. Idempotent: skips if v1.0 exists.
-- The subcommittee roster (sscc_bylaws_members) is added directly against
-- the live project, never committed: this repo is public and the roster
-- is members' personal email addresses (same rule as bios102_students).
do $seed$
begin
if exists (select 1 from sscc_bylaws_versions where num = 1) then return; end if;

Promote community interest, participation, and engagement in the development of cultural, artistic, musical, recreational, social, and educational programs at the South Shore Cultural Center.

Provide a forum for discussion of concerns, interests, needs, and opportunities related to the South Shore Cultural Center and the arts.

Advise, make recommendations to, and work collaboratively with the Chicago Park District (CPD) regarding the operations, programs, preservation, and improvement of the Center.

Assist with fundraising and advocate for the preservation, maintenance, beautification, and appropriate stewardship of the Center.

Promote the growth and development of the Center as a community, regional, and citywide cultural facility.

Encourage long-range planning and responsible stewardship for the continued vitality of the Center.$q$);

Membership requires submission of the Council’s membership application and payment of applicable dues within the membership year. A Family or Organization shall designate in writing one primary representative and one alternate authorized to represent that membership.

Membership shall be renewed annually.$q$);

Any question concerning whether a CPD employee may serve at a particular Park Advisory Council, including a Council serving a park in which the employee resides or works, shall be determined in accordance with current CPD policy and, when necessary, referred to the appropriate CPD office for clarification.$q$);

Act in the best interests of the Council and the South Shore Cultural Center.

Respect the Council’s adopted bylaws, policies, decisions, meeting procedures, and the authority of the presiding officer.

Represent the Council accurately, professionally, respectfully, and in a manner consistent with its mission when acting in an official capacity or identifying themselves as representing the Council.

Refrain from representing personal positions, unauthorized commitments, or individual disputes as official Council positions.

Protect confidential information entrusted to the Council and refrain from using Council information, resources, contacts, or property for unauthorized personal or outside purposes.

Disclose actual or potential conflicts of interest when required and refrain from using Council office or membership for improper personal benefit.

Attend meetings regularly and fulfill responsibilities accepted through election, appointment, committee assignment, or other Council action.$q$);

No person shall be denied an opportunity to seek membership solely because of an inability to pay dues. The Council may establish a consistent and confidential process for addressing financial hardship.$q$);

Members and officers may be subject to corrective action, suspension, removal from office, or termination of membership for conduct that is unlawful, materially inconsistent with these bylaws or CPD requirements, constitutes misuse of Council authority or resources, materially disrupts Council business, or is otherwise detrimental to the Council.

No member or officer shall be removed solely on the basis of an unsupported allegation. Except where immediate action is required by CPD policy or law, the person shall receive written notice of the concern, a reasonable opportunity to respond, and an opportunity to be heard before the final decision.

A written complaint or motion for removal shall identify the conduct at issue and shall be submitted to the Executive Board. The Executive Board shall review the matter and, when appropriate, refer it to a committee or other impartial process for fact-finding. Any officer whose conduct is the subject of the matter shall not preside over or control the review.

Following review, any proposed removal from membership or office shall be presented to the voting membership with the required notice. Removal shall require a two-thirds vote of the votes cast, unless a different requirement is imposed by applicable CPD policy, law, or a later valid bylaw provision.$q$);

Where CPD guidelines provide for review or appeal to the CPD PAC Oversight Committee or another CPD body, the Council shall follow that process. Nothing in these bylaws shall limit or replace a right of appeal or review provided by CPD policy.$q$);

During a declared or significant emergency that prevents an in-person meeting, the Executive Board may take only those emergency actions reasonably necessary to preserve Council operations and comply with CPD requirements. Any such action shall be reported to the membership and entered in the minutes at the next practicable meeting.$q$);

A member shall be considered eligible to vote if the member has attended at least four Council meetings during the twelve-month period preceding the election or bylaw vote, unless CPD requirements establish a different standard.$q$);

The Secretary shall maintain the adopted version and amendment history and shall make the current bylaws available to the membership, the public when required, and the Chicago Park District.$q$);

President

Vice President

Secretary

Treasurer

These officers shall constitute the Executive Board.$q$);

Faithfully perform the duties of the office and comply with these bylaws, Council decisions, CPD requirements, and applicable law.

Attend Executive Board and membership meetings and communicate promptly when unable to attend.

Prepare for meetings and complete assigned follow-up actions within established deadlines.

Maintain accurate records and transfer all Council records, property, passwords, funds, and materials to the Council when leaving office.

Avoid unilateral commitments, expenditures, public statements, contracts, or representations on behalf of the Council unless authorized.

Disclose conflicts of interest and refrain from using office for personal, financial, political, or other unauthorized advantage.

Provide timely reports to the membership and support transparency, continuity, and orderly transition.$q$);

No Council funds shall be disbursed without appropriate authorization. The Council should maintain reasonable separation of financial duties and documentation to protect both the Council and its officers.$q$);

No candidate for an office shall serve on the Nominating Committee while the committee is considering nominations for that office.$q$);

Standing committees shall meet at least every two months unless their work is completed or the Executive Board determines that a different schedule is appropriate. Chairs shall provide notice of committee meetings to the membership, submit reports at regular membership meetings, and provide written reports or records to the Secretary.$q$);

Building and Grounds Committee: Reviews conditions of park facilities, equipment, and surrounding parkland and recommends repair, landscaping, renovation, replacement, maintenance, or beautification.

Program Committee: Assesses programming needs, develops recommendations to CPD, and develops and organizes cultural programs and events approved by the Council.

Fundraising Committee: Develops and supports lawful fundraising, sponsorship, grant, and other resource-development activities to support Council-approved programming and community needs.

Membership and Community Outreach Committee: Recruits and engages members, supports outreach at Park events, maintains an up-to-date membership roll, and provides membership information to the Secretary.

Marketing and Technical Committee: Maintains Council-approved social media and communications content and supports virtual meetings and related technology.$q$);

The proposed amendment shall be provided to members with the notice of the meeting at which it will be considered, and no amendment shall be adopted without the required previous notice.

A two-thirds vote of the votes cast, with a quorum present, shall be required for adoption unless applicable CPD requirements or law require a higher standard.$q$);
end;
$seed$;
